WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
Batch size tuning for inference is the practice of choosing how much work the runtime

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.725
should process together when it serves prompts and generates tokens.

00:00:07.725 --> 00:00:11.975
It sounds simple, but it sits at the intersection of model size, context length, memory,

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:13.580
hardware, and user expectations.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.720
In practical terms,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.400
batch settings decide whether the system handles one request at a time,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.610
groups pieces of work together,

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.460
or keeps multiple active requests moving through the model at once.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.320
This is different from training batch size.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.990
During training, a batch is tied to examples, loss calculation, gradients,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.880
and weight updates.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.555
During inference, the model weights are already fixed.

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:33.100
The question becomes operational:

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.880
how can the runtime use available compute efficiently while still returning useful

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:37.780
responses quickly?

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.020
A setting that makes sense for training documentation may not translate directly to a chat

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.610
server or a local assistant.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.830
It also helps to separate prompt processing from token generation.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.050
When a prompt is submitted,

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.300
the runtime has to process the existing context before the first new token appears.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.960
After that, generation usually happens step by step.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.270
Batch settings can affect these phases differently,

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.120
so a configuration that improves prompt ingestion may not produce the smoothest streaming

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:01.560
response.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.590
The right answer depends on the workload.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:08.250
A single-user local coding assistant usually values responsiveness and stable memory use.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.980
A multi-user API service may accept slightly more latency if it can increase total

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:12.760
throughput.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.055
There is no universal best batch size because the best operating point changes with model

00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:17.540
architecture,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.640
quantization, runtime, hardware, prompt length, concurrency, and reliability goals.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.965
A practical way to think about this is to start with the user experience you want,

00:01:24.965 --> 00:01:27.255
then work backward to the runtime settings.

00:01:27.255 --> 00:01:28.720
If the system is a private assistant,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.685
the tuning target may be quick feedback and steady streaming.

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:35.535
If it is an internal API, the target may be more completed requests per minute.

00:01:35.535 --> 00:01:39.280
Batch size is only meaningful when it is tied to one of those operating goals.
